+# Owned CPU-job recovery increment
+
+This isolated source increment derives from the sealed `owned-hub-jobs` packet, index SHA256 `a0b6be34cab423581e8f9a80efd42791e17946cc96ff7e7e5226112e1273b229`. `parent-pins.json` and `baseline/` retain exact original production/control/runner/helper bytes. The original packet and public HMS sources remain unchanged. This increment has not been executed.
+
+The typed fixed-artifact API, one active job, sixteen retained keys, gate association, strict result validation, writer lease, pidfd/unreaped-anchor signal authority and single-thread/default-SIGCHLD/no-other-reaper contract are retained. Historical original-key replay remains before current identity or poisoned-storage checks. A new key is explicitly refused when control storage is poisoned.
+
+The correction addresses a compound failure: a terminal commit can fail while an original process has already met all settlement conditions; a synchronous exception after reap can then leave its live object retained while the durable row still says released. Ordinary stop/poll code cannot re-anchor an already-reaped child. `poll`, `cancel` and `reconcile` now finish a retained object's previously established settlement before entering any stop/signal path. That branch uses only the existing object and its settlement proof, never a persisted PID. `reconcile` also releases retained handles for a terminal receipt. Successful cleanup does not repair or retry poisoned ledger writes and does not manufacture a durable completed result.
+
+The ledger adds private no-op checkpoints before/after file fsync, replacement, directory fsync and in-memory publication. Installed controls inject `ENOSPC` exceptions at those boundaries for terminal commits. These are exact code-path fault injections, not real disk exhaustion or power failures. A failed commit poisons current launch authority regardless of how far it advanced. If a subsequent owner reads only the pre-replacement released row, reconciliation produces `unknown` with no result or exit code. If a valid terminal row reached the committed slot and remains readable after reopen, its already-validated receipt is retained. Neither case reconstructs success from lost stdout. Directory fsync success in a control is not a device power-loss durability qualification.
+
+Compound controls pair each of eight storage boundaries with no reap interruption, a synchronous before-reap exception, a synchronous post-reap exception, and a pending post-reap SIGINT. They assert retained-handle settlement, refusal of a fresh key under poison, unchanged original-key replay and the conservative reopened outcome. A separate terminal-reconcile case verifies that its published receipt remains byte-equivalent while the retained handle is released.
+
